ers' "not introduced" lists accordingly:  - Item 3: the byte-literal lexeme `'c'` with c ASCII printable (decide and state precisely which ASCII scalars are allowed raw, consistent with item 4: no raw control characters) or an escape; non-ASCII refused; `'label` vs `'a'` distinguished by the closing quote, with tests for both (e.g. `'a'`, `'a`, `'ab` as a label if labels are identifier-shaped, `'é'` refused). THE ESCAPE LIST IS NOT DECIDED ("its own small escape list" gives no members): do not choose members. Parameterize the byte-literal escape table exactly as the string escapes are handled, prove the theorems for any table, instantiate no members beyond what docs/decisions.md or the spec already decide for byte literals (search; cite what you find), and report the open question.  - Item 4: string bodies refuse raw tab and every other control character (state which: C0 controls and DEL? cite the decided rule; if only "control character" is decided, use Unicode general category Cc = U+0000-U+001F and U+007F-U+009F and say so in the report as a point to confirm), with the refusal as an internal error constructor.  - Item 5: integer lexemes with optional 0x/0b/0o prefix (digits valid for the base, at least one digit; decide nothing about uppercase 0X/hex-digit case unless decided — search docs/decisions.md; if undecided, accept only what item 5 states and report), the existing leading-zero refusal kept for decimal, `_` refused, and a suffix: a maximal run of identifier-continue scalars directly after the digits is the suffix candidate and must be one of the decided integer type names (find the decided integer types in docs/decisions.md / the spec; cite them); any other candidate (123u9, 12abc, 0x1g) is a lexing error, never split into number + identifier.  OWNER RULE (2026-11-08, verbatim: "ALWAYS tell the reviewrs to do the best long term thing rather than cuitting corners"): judge and fix by the best long-term design. Never cut corners for speed, a smaller diff or fewer rounds. As a reviewer, report any shortcut, containment patch or corner cut as a real finding and say what the long-term fix is. As a writer, implement the structural fix, not a containment patch; if staging is truly unavoidable, record the long-term design and name its roadmap row.  Run inside the repository's nix devshell: build only the touched Lean modules (lake build Concrete.Lex ConcreteSpec.Spec.Surface0 or the narrowest targets), bash scripts/proofs/axioms.sh or the axioms golden check the branch uses, and the lexer's own checks if any (grep scripts/checks for Lex). Bound every run; no full suites. Do not edit docs/decisions.md or ROADMAP.md. Add changelog.d/2026-11-08-lexer-literals.md citing owner items 3, 4 and 5. Commit on wip/lexer-literals in logical commits (do not push). If a rule needs a decision not in the text above or the repository, stop and report it rather than choose. End with a short report: what was added, theorems, tests, open questions (escape list, control-character set, prefix case, anything else).
