thing missed. Do not modify files. Cite repository paths you read.  End with exactly one line: ANSWER: AGREE=<points>; DISAGREE=<points or none>; EXPLAIN=<command|other: what>; CASCADE=<rule in a few words>; BEFORE_SLICE1=<yes|no>; MISSED=<short list or none>  Decided context: diagnostics are typed checker results; a refusal says false (with witness) or not established; rule ID = Lean rule name + per-rule revision (e.g. StmtOk.let_.admitted@2), no numeric codes; fixes offered only after full recheck, auto-applied only if a lemma proves meaning preservation; NO warnings (weaker findings are facts, policies may refuse); output human text on a terminal and JSON lines otherwise (--format=human|json) from one record; frozen versioned JSON schema with goldens from the first front-end output (R-0593); byte columns, tab = one byte; trap line 'concrete: trap: <kind> at <path>:<line>:<col> (site <id>)'; tokens carry kind+payload+span; pipeline review proposed typed SiteId/span/origin edges minted at lex/parse and preserved to BIR.  === K13.md === # K13. Diagnostics at Elm quality (owner, 2026-11-08, verbatim: "i want to have elm like errors for item 1 so we need to make sure they are good"; item 1 = K1 source provenance)  Claude's proposal, to be judged against Elm, rustc, Gleam and Roc: a. Diagnostics as structured data with one renderer: each diagnostic carries a title, the rule ID (Lean rule name + revision), a primary span, labelled secondary spans ("moved here", "used again here"), a plain-language explanation and an optional fix hint. The same record renders as Elm-style terminal text and as JSON lines for agents (decided diagnostics row; K12; R-0593). b. What provenance (K1) must carry: spans with both ends (byte offsets plus line/column; decided: byte columns, a tab counts one byte), related spans across passes (a refusal found after elaboration or Mono still points at written source), and type-origin tracking ("this is u8 because of here"). K1's design must supply all of it. c. One explanation page per rule ID, with a failing example and its fixed version, generated from the rule's Lean doc comment and conformance examples (no second prose source, rule 5/6). A `concrete explain <rule>` command is new CLI surface: an owner question (one way per thing). d. A message style guide: second person, no unexplained jargon, show the code, say what to do next. A hint appear
